// A view of part of an array.
//
// **Invented here**, not agreed. Seven rewritten packages pass `(bytes, lo, hi)` triples around —
// `atofSpan(u8[] src, i32 start, i32 end)`, `slice(query, at, eq)`, `isValidTarget(s, lo, hi)` — and
// `packages/bytes`'s `slice` copies, so the alternative to the triple is an allocation per token.
// A parser that allocates per token is a parser that allocates per byte of input.
//
// Two arguments that must travel together and must not be swapped is a struct. That is all this is.
//
// ## The rules, and why each is the one it is
//
// **A `T[]` widens to a `Slice<T>` implicitly.** The same widening as `T` to `T?`: safe, unchecked,
// and one direction only. A function should be able to say it reads a run of bytes without every
// caller writing a conversion, and an array is a slice of the whole of itself.
//
// **Indexing traps.** `s[i]` is `a[i]` with an offset, and the caller of either usually knows the
// index is good — the rule `Vec.pop` is on the other side of.
//
// **`slice` traps too**, and for *two* reasons — the second measured 2026-09-05 after a rewrite
// claimed a refusing variant was the missing piece and was wrong.
//
// The first is the stated one: the bounds come from a scan the caller just did. Five of the eight
// `slice` call sites in `packages/` are that — `@/packages/url`'s `eq` and `at` from a scan of one
// pair, `@/packages/http`'s `eol` from `findCrlf`, `@/packages/regex`'s own match bounds,
// `@/packages/tor`'s after a length check, `@/packages/stream`'s held tail.
//
// The other three are **input-derived**, which is exactly the case the stated reason does not cover:
// `@/packages/abi`'s `blobAt` reads a length word out of attacker-controlled data, and
// `@/packages/rlp`'s `take` reads one off the wire. A trap there would be a denial of service where a
// refusal is wanted, so both check first and neither reaches the trap.
//
// **And a nullable `take(lo, hi)` would help neither.** Both produce a fault carrying numbers —
// `LengthOverruns(at, n, end - at - 32)` and `Truncated(this.at, n, this.src.len() - this.at)` — and
// a `null` cannot say how far over the length ran. So the check stays in the package that knows what
// the numbers mean, and the trap stays for the callers that cannot reach it.
//
// The rule underneath, which is not the one this file gave: a trapping bound is right when the
// caller either **knows** the bounds or **wants to describe** why they were wrong. Only a caller who
// would be content with *no* needs the nullable form, and there is not one here.
//
// **A slice does not own.** It keeps its array alive and nothing more. Writing through a non-const
// slice writes through to the array, and a `const` one cannot write — deep const already says so.
//
// ## What it costs, which is not nothing, and I claimed nothing
//
// `spec/spec/structs.md`: *"Structs are WasmGC struct types — heap-allocated, garbage-collected
// references."* So every `s.slice(lo, hi)` is a `struct.new`. This file said *"there is no copy
// anywhere in this file, which is the whole point"* and `url/query.wac` said parsing a query
// *"allocates nothing at all"*. No copy is true. Nothing allocated is false.
//
// The honest trade is **an O(1) allocation in place of an O(n) allocation and a copy**. Against
// `packages/bytes`'s `slice`, which copies, that is a straight win and a large one for long spans.
//
// **Against a `(bytes, lo, hi)` triple it is a loss.** A triple allocates nothing at all —
// `atofSpan(u8[] src, i32 start, i32 end)` passes three machine words — and handing it a `Bytes`
// adds an allocation where there was none. Every parser in this rewrite that took a triple and now
// takes a slice pays for that.
//
// **And now it is measured rather than argued.** `../bench/slicecost.wac` compiles a `View` struct
// in today's wac, which is what `Slice<u8>` would compile to, and sweeps the window:
//
//      win     calls   triple     view    fresh    again
//        8   8388608       48       53       71       50
//       32   2097152       41       43       47       41
//      128    524288       40       40       42       40
//     1024     65536       41       41       41       41
//
// A fresh view is **2.6 ns on the v8 host** — the bench measures one engine and says why that
// matters: *"A different collector could move the allocation number and would not move the shape,
// since the shape is per-call against per-byte."* One `struct.new` of three fields. Reusing one is
// 0.5 ns. So the loss
// is 45% at an eight-byte call, 15% at 32, 5% at 128, and nothing at a kilobyte — which sorts the
// consumers rather than settling the type: a DEFLATE match is 3–258 bytes and mostly short, a JSON
// token and an HTTP header value are tens of bytes, a read buffer is thousands. The bench header has
// the rest.
//
// The number bounds the cost; it does not rescue it. Which leaves the argument for this type
// standing where it already stood, on **correctness rather than cost**: two arguments that must
// travel together cannot be given the bounds of one buffer and the bytes of another. That was always
// the better half of the case, and it was not the half being made.
//
// ## When to hold one, which is not always
//
// A slice cannot dangle: the collector keeps the array alive for as long as any view of it. So the
// first question is **retention** — a `Slice<u8>` of six bytes holds the megabyte it was cut from,
// and holds it until the slice goes too. (This paragraph said *"the question is never safety"* and
// that is wrong; the section after this one is the safety question, and it was found by writing a
// package rather than by reading this file.)
//
// That makes the rule a lifetime judgement rather than a preference:
//
//   - **A view where the value lives for a call.** `Request.method` and `Request.target` are read
//     while the connection buffer is in hand and are gone before the next request, so parsing an
//     HTTP request can allocate nothing at all.
//   - **An owned array where the value outlives the buffer.** `JsonValue.Str` is part of a tree that
//     a caller keeps, and one string field held as a view would pin the whole document. A parser
//     that returns a tree copies; a parser that answers a question does not.
//
// Getting that backwards is not a crash. It is a program that holds ten megabytes to remember a
// hostname, which is the failure mode worth naming because nothing will report it.
//
// ## And there is a second one, which is the mirror of it
//
// Retention is holding a view too long. The other way to be wrong is **overwriting what a view
// points at, too soon** — and it is the more dangerous of the two, because retention wastes memory
// and this returns wrong bytes.
//
// `vision/packages/gzip/src/window.wac` found it by being written. A sliding window hands its front
// away and then compacts, and the shipped decoder allocates a fresh array for the hand-off. Replace
// that with a view of the window's own storage and the compaction in the next line overwrites it:
// the caller receives the right length, the right type, and the following window's bytes.
//
// **The shipped code cannot have that bug, and not because it is careful.** It copies, so nothing
// aliases the buffer and there is nothing to invalidate. Take the copy away — which is the whole
// point of this type — and an aliasing rule appears with nothing in the language holding it.
//
// So a view into a buffer that is *reused* needs a stated point at which the holder is finished
// with it. A coroutine has one: the holder has the chunk for exactly as long as the machine is
// suspended, and a resumed generator is that point. A callback does not — `sink(out)` returns before
// its caller is necessarily done with the bytes.
//
// This is not an argument against the type. It is the rule the type needs and the one this file
// did not have: **a view of a private buffer is safe when the buffer is append-only, and needs a
// release point when it is not.**

export struct Slice<T> {
  T[] of;
  i32 from;
  i32 len;

  /** The whole array, which is what the implicit widening produces. */
  Slice<T> all(T[] a) { return Slice(a, 0, a.len()); }

  /**
   * A view over nothing.
   *
   * `Slice(T[0](), 0, 0)` at every site otherwise, and `@/packages/tty` has five of them in one
   * file — *nothing to draw* is the ordinary answer of a line discipline. Added when the fifth
   * appeared rather than the second, which is the rule this directory has been using for `core`.
   */
  Slice<T> empty() { return Slice(T[0](), 0, 0); }

  i32 len(const this) { return this.len; }

  T get(const this, i32 i) {
    // Not `this.of[this.from + i]` alone: an `i` past `len` would read the array beyond the view
    // and answer a byte that is not in this slice. That is the bug a triple makes easy and a type
    // is supposed to remove, so the bound is checked here rather than inherited.
    if (i < 0 || i >= this.len) { trap "Slice.get out of range"; }
    return this.of[this.from + i];
  }

  void set(this, i32 i, T v) {
    if (i < 0 || i >= this.len) { trap "Slice.set out of range"; }
    this.of[this.from + i] = v;
  }

  /** A narrower view of the same array. No copy. */
  Slice<T> slice(const this, i32 lo, i32 hi) {
    if (lo < 0 || hi > this.len || lo > hi) { trap "Slice.slice out of range"; }
    return Slice(this.of, this.from + lo, hi - lo);
  }

  Slice<T> from(const this, i32 lo) { return this.slice(lo, this.len); }

  gen<T> void items(const this) {
    for (i32 i = 0; i < this.len; i++) { yield this.of[this.from + i]; }
  }

  /** The copy, when a caller actually wants to keep the bytes past the array's life. */
  T[] toArray(const this) { … }

  /**
   * Equal contents — **and it takes a comparator, which is the whole finding.**
   *
   * Four call sites wanted this and none could have it. `@/packages/box/src/lines.wac`:
   * *"`cmpBytes(a, b) == 0` is how equality is spelled … `Slice` has no `eq`"*, and it is in
   * `@/packages/box/src/applets/diff.wac`'s inner loop, **twice per iteration**, where a three-way
   * comparison answers a yes/no question and walks the whole slice for the common case of unequal
   * lengths. `@/packages/tor/src/onionaddr.wac` found the same absence and wrote two `get`s.
   * `./map.wac` needed a `bytesEq` and declared its own.
   *
   * The length check alone is most of the win — two slices of different lengths are unequal in one
   * comparison, and `cmpBytes` cannot know that because a three-way answer has to say *which is
   * smaller* and that needs the bytes.
   *
   * **`T` has no equality, so this cannot be `eq(const this, const Slice<T> other)`.** There are no
   * traits and no constraints on type parameters, so a generic `Slice<T>` cannot ask `T` whether two
   * of them are the same. The comparator is not a convenience here; it is the only spelling.
   */
  bool eq(const this, const Slice<T> other, fn<bool(T, T)> same) {
    if (this.len != other.len) { return false; }
    for (i32 i = 0; i < this.len; i++) {
      if (!same(this.of[this.from + i], other.of[other.from + i])) { return false; }
    }
    return true;
  }
}

/**
 * Byte equality, and the reason it is a free function rather than a method on `Bytes`.
 *
 * `Bytes` is `Slice<u8>`, an *instantiation*, and a method cannot be added to one instantiation of a
 * generic struct — `Slice<T>.eq` is the only place a method can live and it does not know that `T`
 * is `u8`. So the specialisation that needs no comparator has to be a function beside the type.
 *
 * Which is the cost of `export Slice<u8> Bytes;` being a name for a type rather than a type: it
 * cannot carry its own operations. `@/packages/core/map.wac`'s `Key<Bytes> bytes()` has the mirror
 * problem — a static whose type parameter is fixed — and both are the same missing thing.
 */
export bool bytesEq(const Bytes a, const Bytes b) {
  return a.eq(b, (u8 x, u8 y) => x == y);
}

/**
 * A one-byte slice — **the operation the body-hash sweep found written three times here.**
 *
 * `@/packages/rlp/src/decode.wac` allocates a `u8[1]` and wraps it; `@/packages/tty/src/line.wac`
 * and `@/packages/tty/src/render.wac` push into a `Buf` and take its bytes, identically. Three
 * spellings of *a byte, as a slice*, and none of the three authors could have found the others by
 * grepping for a name, since all three are called `one`.
 *
 * Three lines in `core` rather than three copies, and it is the small half of that sweep's finding —
 * the large half is that the nine shipped byte-concatenations have **no** counterpart here, because
 * a `Bytes` removed the reason to write one. This one the type did not remove: a single byte really
 * does have to become a slice somewhere.
 */
export Bytes oneByte(u8 b) {
  u8[] a = u8[1]();
  a[0] = b;
  return Bytes.all(a);
}

/**
 * Byte slices are common enough to deserve the short name.
 *
 * Same form as `vision/packages/http/src/fault.wac`'s `export union<A, B> RequestFault;` — one way
 * to give a type a name, whether the type is a union or an instantiation. Whether such a name is a
 * distinct type or an alias is the same question for both, which is an argument for the one form.
 */
export Slice<u8> Bytes;
